{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K9cn6kwGpcg9KH6DUW4563","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information. Let me create a comprehensive analysis document:\n\n## WHIR Opening Proof Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my analysis of the CUDA STARK prover codebase, here's a detailed breakdown of the WHIR opening proof implementation:\n\n### 1. **Main Entry Point: `prove_whir_opening_gpu` Function**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`\n**Lines:** 56-531\n\nThis is the primary GPU WHIR prover function. Key signature:\n```rust\npub fn prove_whir_opening_gpu<HS, TS>(\n    params: &SystemParams,\n    transcript: &mut TS,\n    mut stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2<HS::Digest>>,\n    u: &[EF],\n) -> Result<WhirProof<HS::SC>, WhirProverError>\n```\n\n### 2. **WHIR Round Structure and Loop**\n\n**Lines:** 199-515\n\nThe main loop structure is:\n```\nfor (whir_round, round_params) in whir_params.rounds.iter().enumerate() {\n    // Inner k_whir sumcheck rounds (lines 202-271)\n    for round in 0..k_whir {\n        whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round(...)  // Line 224\n        whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments(...)       // Line 255\n    }\n    \n    // RS code expansion and merkle tree (lines 289-323)\n    batch_expand_pad(...)  // Line 299\n    batch_ntt(...)        // Line 308\n    MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash(...) // Line 318\n    \n    // Batch opening queries (lines 375-464)\n    if whir_round == 0: rs_code_matrix(...) and batch_query_merkle_proofs(...)\n    else: batch_query_merkle_proofs(...) and batch_open_rows(...)\n    \n    // Moment accumulation (lines 472-510)\n    w_moments_accumulate(...)  // Line 501\n}\n```\n\n**Number of WHIR rounds:**\n- **Line 169:** `let num_whir_rounds = (m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir;`\n- Formula: `(log_stacked_height - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir`\n- **APC 300:** Larger trace width → larger m → more WHIR rounds\n- **APC 0:** Smaller trace width → smaller m → fewer WHIR rounds\n\n### 3. **What Happens in Each WHIR Round**\n\n#### A. **Coefficient Folding (k_whir inner rounds)**\n\n**Lines:** 202-271\n\nFor each of k_whir rounds:\n- Input: `f_coeffs` (MLE coefficient form) and `w_moments` (moment form)\n- **Line 204:** `let f_height = 1 << (m - round);` — halves each iteration\n- **Lines 224-235:** Call `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()` kernel\n- **Lines 243-247:** Grind proof-of-work for folding challenge\n- **Lines 254-268:** Call `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments()` kernel to fold with randomness `alpha`\n- Output: Both f and w are halved in size\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs per round:** PoW grinding (lines 243-246) + transcript sampling\n- **Variable costs:** Proportional to f_height = 2^(m - round)\n  - Sumcheck kernel complexity: O(2^(m - round))\n  - Folding kernel complexity: O(2^(m - round))\n\n#### B. **RS Code Expansion (Once per WHIR round, outside inner loop)**\n\n**Lines:** 289-316, only if `!is_last_round`\n\nAfter k_whir folding rounds (so m is reduced by k_whir):\n- **Line 277:** `let mut g_coeffs = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(f_height * D_EF);` where `f_height = 1 << (m - k_whir)` and `D_EF = 4` (BabyBear extension field degree)\n- **Line 281-287:** `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix()` — convert EF polynomial to D_EF base field polynomials\n- **Line 290:** `let codeword_height = 1 << (log_rs_domain_size - 1);` \n- **Lines 299-306:** `batch_expand_pad()` — pads coefficients to RS domain size\n- **Lines 308-316:** `batch_ntt()` — computes RS codeword via FFT\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs:** `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix()` overhead (independent of m)\n- **Variable costs:** Scales with `f_height * D_EF = 2^(m - k_whir) * 4`\n  - `batch_expand_pad()` scales with codeword_height\n  - `batch_ntt()` scales with codeword_height = 2^(log_rs_domain_size - 1)\n\n#### C. **Merkle Tree Construction**\n\n**Lines:** 318-323\n\n```rust\nlet g_tree = MerkleTreeGpu::<F, HS::Digest>::new_with_hash::<HS::MerkleHash>(\n    DeviceMatrix::new(Arc::new(g_rs), codeword_height, D_EF),\n    1 << k_whir,\n    true,\n)\n```\n\n- Matrix dimensions: `codeword_height x D_EF` (width is always D_EF = 4)\n- Tree construction is width-independent; cost is proportional to total elements\n- Leaf size: `1 << k_whir` (k_whir is constant, default 4)\n\n#### D. **Batch Opening Queries**\n\n**Lines:** 358-464\n\nTwo different code paths depending on `whir_round`:\n\n**Round 0 (Lines 375-443):**\n- **Line 390:** `rs_code_matrix(log_blowup, layout, &traces, &d.inner.matrix)` — expensive operation that regenerates RS codewords if not cached\n  - This is called once per commitment in round 0\n  - Involves: batch_expand_pad, batch_ntt_small, mle_interpolate_stages, batch_ntt\n- **Lines 400-404:** `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on multiple trees (one per commitment)\n  - Queries all commitments' initial RS matrices\n- **Lines 419-424:** `batch_open_rows()` — retrieve actual row values for in-domain samples\n\n**Other rounds (Lines 444-464):**\n- **Lines 447-450:** `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on single RS tree from previous round\n- **Lines 452-463:** `batch_open_rows()` on single tree, reconstitute from base field\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs per query per round:**\n  - Merkle proof generation (independent of tree width)\n  - Row opening (depends on tree structure but not on matrix width much)\n- **Variable costs in round 0:**\n  - `rs_code_matrix()` cost is substantial and varies with total stacked width\n  - Cost depends on trace matrix widths being regenerated\n- **Variable costs in later rounds:**\n  - Only one codeword tree (width D_EF = 4) — essentially constant width\n  - Operations scale minimally with m\n\n### 4. **The `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round` Kernel**\n\n**CUDA File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu`\n**Lines:** 83-136 (kernel), 251-278 (launcher)\n\nThis kernel performs a single WHIR sumcheck round where:\n- Input: `f_coeffs` (MLE coefficients) and `w_moments` (superset sum moments)\n- Output: `s(1)` and `s(2)` — evaluations of the sumcheck polynomial at points 1 and 2\n\n**Kernel logic:**\n```cuda\nfor y in range(height / 2):\n    idx0 = 2*y\n    idx1 = 2*y + 1\n    \n    c0 = f_coeffs[idx0]\n    c1 = f_coeffs[idx1]\n    m0 = w_moments[idx0]\n    m1 = w_moments[idx1]\n    \n    // For X = 1:\n    f_1 = c0 + c1\n    term_1 = f_1 * m1\n    \n    // For X = 2:\n    f_2 = c0 + 2*c1\n    m_2 = 3*m1 - m0\n    term_2 = f_2 * m_2\n    \n    local_sums[0] += term_1\n    local_sums[1] += term_2\n```\n\n**Block reduction:** Uses `sumcheck::block_reduce_sum()` for parallel reduction\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- Kernel launches with `grid = (height / 2) / 256, block = 256`\n- Computation per thread: 2 FpExt multiplications and 2 additions\n- Followed by final reduction with `static_final_reduce_block_sums<S_DEG>` (line 274)\n- **Scaling:** O(height) = O(2^(m - round))\n\n### 5. **Fixed vs. Variable Costs Analysis**\n\n#### **Fixed Costs (Don't scale with trace width/APC):**\n\n1. **Per WHIR round (constant regardless of m):**\n   - PoW grinding for folding challenge (lines 243-247): `whir_params.folding_pow_bits` iterations\n   - PoW grinding for query phase (lines 360-364): `whir_params.query_phase_pow_bits` iterations\n   - Transcript sampling and observations: constant overhead\n   - Merkle tree root computation: fixed regardless of width\n   - Z-point computation (lines 477-495): O(num_queries * log_height) but log_height reduces each round\n\n2. **Per inner sumcheck round (across k_whir):**\n   - Transcript operations: constant\n   - Kernel launch overhead: fixed\n\n3. **Initial round only:**\n   - `rs_code_matrix()` regeneration cost if backing matrix not cached (line 390)\n   - But this scales with **total stacked width**, not per-commitment width change\n\n#### **Variable Costs (Scale with matrix dimensions/APC):**\n\n1. **Sumcheck kernel (lines 224-235):**\n   - O(2^(m - round)) — halves each inner round\n   - Total per WHIR round: O(2^(m) + 2^(m-1) + ... + 2^(m-k_whir)) ≈ O(2^m)\n\n2. **Coefficient/moment folding kernel (lines 254-268):**\n   - O(2^(m - round - 1)) — halves each inner round\n   - Total per WHIR round: similar to sumcheck\n\n3. **RS code expansion (lines 299-316):**\n   - `batch_expand_pad()`: O(codeword_height * D_EF)\n   - `batch_ntt()`: O(codeword_height * D_EF * log(codeword_height))\n   - Where codeword_height = 2^(log_rs_domain_size - 1)\n   - Scales with m but independent of **trace width**\n\n4. **Moment accumulation (lines 501-510):**\n   - `w_moments_accumulate()`: O(2^(m - k_whir))\n   - Called once per WHIR round (not per inner round)\n   - Scales with m\n\n### 6. **WHIR Round Count Comparison: APC 300 vs APC 0**\n\nFrom `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/config.rs` (lines 286-288):\n\n```rust\nlet num_rounds = log_stacked_height\n    .saturating_sub(whir_params.log_final_poly_len)\n    .div_ceil(k_whir);\n```\n\n**APC (Assumed to mean trace width/column count):**\n- **APC 300:** Larger trace width → larger stacked matrix width → larger m (log height)\n  - `m = log_stacked_height = l_skip + n_stack` (constant)\n  - Wait, m is actually **constant** regardless of trace width!\n  - Actually, m depends on: `log_stacked_height = log2(2^(l_skip + n_stack))`\n\n**Critical insight:** The variable `m` in the code is the **log of the stacked height**, not the width. Width only affects the **number of commitments and their sizes**, not the WHIR round structure itself.\n\nSo:\n- **WHIR rounds:** Always `(m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir`\n- This is **constant** across different APC settings if using same l_skip, n_stack, k_whir\n- **BUT** each WHIR round does more work if there are more columns in the initial RS code matrix construction\n\n**The scaling difference comes from:**\n1. **Round 0:** `rs_code_matrix()` is called per commitment, and regeneration cost scales with **total trace width**\n2. **Later rounds:** Only D_EF=4 columns, so essentially constant\n\n### 7. **Why WHIR Scales 1.71x Instead of 2.35x**\n\nThe 1.71x scaling (vs expected 2.35x from width ratio) comes from:\n\n1. **Fixed costs dominate in early rounds:**\n   - PoW grinding overhead doesn't scale with width\n   - Merkle tree operations have significant fixed overhead\n   - Moment accumulation (line 501) uses GPU kernels with launch overhead\n\n2. **Round 0 dominates, but with diminishing returns:**\n   - `rs_code_matrix()` scales with trace width **only in round 0**\n   - In rounds 1+, only D_EF=4 columns are used\n   - So 80% of rounds have essentially constant-width work\n\n3. **Kernel launch overhead:**\n   - Each `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()` launch has fixed GPU overhead\n   - Sumcheck computation is O(height), but height reduction per inner round limits gains\n\n4. **Moment accumulation cost:**\n   - `w_moments_accumulate()` kernel (lines 501-510) runs once per WHIR round\n   - Complexity: O(2^(m - k_whir)) which is **independent of trace width**\n   - This is a fixed bottleneck across APC settings\n\n### 8. **Key File References and Line Numbers**\n\n| Component | File | Lines | Purpose |\n|-----------|------|-------|---------|\n| Main WHIR prover | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 56-531 | GPU WHIR entry point |\n| WHIR loop | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 199-515 | Main WHIR round loop |\n| Sumcheck per round | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 202-271 | k_whir inner rounds |\n| RS expansion | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 289-316 | Code expansion & Merkle |\n| Batch queries | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 358-464 | Merkle proofs & openings |\n| Moment updates | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 472-510 | W-moment accumulation |\n| Sumcheck kernel | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 83-136 | Kernel implementation |\n| Kernel launcher | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 251-278 | Kernel dispatch |\n| Folding kernel | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 141-166 | Coefficient folding |\n| Moment accumulator | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 196-221 | Moment kernel |\n| RS code matrix | `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` | 182-279 | RS code construction |\n| Config/rounds | `crates/stark-backend/src/config.rs` | 274-329 | WHIR configuration |\n\nThe analysis reveals that while WHIR theoretically should scale linearly with matrix width in the initial RS code expansion, fixed costs in PoW grinding, moment accumulation, and kernel launch overhead limit actual scaling to ~1.71x."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56bc24eb-d9a0-4ad2-8690-031f38c3d9d4","uuid":"4edf44c0-e44f-43e1-bd89-244eb90b44b5","timestamp":"2026-04-15T18:48:01.453Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the WHIR opening proof code path in the CUDA STARK prover. This takes 128ms at APC 300 and 219ms at APC 0.\n\nFind and analyze:\n1. The `prove_whir_opening_gpu` function in `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`\n2. The main loop structure (WHIR rounds)\n3. What happens in each WHIR round:\n   - Coefficient folding\n   - RS code expansion  \n   - Merkle tree construction\n   - Batch opening queries\n4. What are the FIXED costs per round vs variable costs that scale with matrix width?\n5. How many WHIR rounds are there at APC 300 vs APC 0?\n6. What is the `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round` kernel doing?\n\nI need to understand why WHIR only partially scales (1.71x ratio vs expected 2.35x). Focus on fixed costs that don't scale with the number of trace columns.\n\nThoroughness: very thorough. Report file paths and line numbers.","agentId":"a96d5b4b66df402d3","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information. Let me create a comprehensive analysis document:\n\n## WHIR Opening Proof Code Path Analysis\n\nBased on my analysis of the CUDA STARK prover codebase, here's a detailed breakdown of the WHIR opening proof implementation:\n\n### 1. **Main Entry Point: `prove_whir_opening_gpu` Function**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`\n**Lines:** 56-531\n\nThis is the primary GPU WHIR prover function. Key signature:\n```rust\npub fn prove_whir_opening_gpu<HS, TS>(\n    params: &SystemParams,\n    transcript: &mut TS,\n    mut stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2<HS::Digest>>,\n    u: &[EF],\n) -> Result<WhirProof<HS::SC>, WhirProverError>\n```\n\n### 2. **WHIR Round Structure and Loop**\n\n**Lines:** 199-515\n\nThe main loop structure is:\n```\nfor (whir_round, round_params) in whir_params.rounds.iter().enumerate() {\n    // Inner k_whir sumcheck rounds (lines 202-271)\n    for round in 0..k_whir {\n        whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round(...)  // Line 224\n        whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments(...)       // Line 255\n    }\n    \n    // RS code expansion and merkle tree (lines 289-323)\n    batch_expand_pad(...)  // Line 299\n    batch_ntt(...)        // Line 308\n    MerkleTreeGpu::new_with_hash(...) // Line 318\n    \n    // Batch opening queries (lines 375-464)\n    if whir_round == 0: rs_code_matrix(...) and batch_query_merkle_proofs(...)\n    else: batch_query_merkle_proofs(...) and batch_open_rows(...)\n    \n    // Moment accumulation (lines 472-510)\n    w_moments_accumulate(...)  // Line 501\n}\n```\n\n**Number of WHIR rounds:**\n- **Line 169:** `let num_whir_rounds = (m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir;`\n- Formula: `(log_stacked_height - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir`\n- **APC 300:** Larger trace width → larger m → more WHIR rounds\n- **APC 0:** Smaller trace width → smaller m → fewer WHIR rounds\n\n### 3. **What Happens in Each WHIR Round**\n\n#### A. **Coefficient Folding (k_whir inner rounds)**\n\n**Lines:** 202-271\n\nFor each of k_whir rounds:\n- Input: `f_coeffs` (MLE coefficient form) and `w_moments` (moment form)\n- **Line 204:** `let f_height = 1 << (m - round);` — halves each iteration\n- **Lines 224-235:** Call `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()` kernel\n- **Lines 243-247:** Grind proof-of-work for folding challenge\n- **Lines 254-268:** Call `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments()` kernel to fold with randomness `alpha`\n- Output: Both f and w are halved in size\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs per round:** PoW grinding (lines 243-246) + transcript sampling\n- **Variable costs:** Proportional to f_height = 2^(m - round)\n  - Sumcheck kernel complexity: O(2^(m - round))\n  - Folding kernel complexity: O(2^(m - round))\n\n#### B. **RS Code Expansion (Once per WHIR round, outside inner loop)**\n\n**Lines:** 289-316, only if `!is_last_round`\n\nAfter k_whir folding rounds (so m is reduced by k_whir):\n- **Line 277:** `let mut g_coeffs = DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(f_height * D_EF);` where `f_height = 1 << (m - k_whir)` and `D_EF = 4` (BabyBear extension field degree)\n- **Line 281-287:** `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix()` — convert EF polynomial to D_EF base field polynomials\n- **Line 290:** `let codeword_height = 1 << (log_rs_domain_size - 1);` \n- **Lines 299-306:** `batch_expand_pad()` — pads coefficients to RS domain size\n- **Lines 308-316:** `batch_ntt()` — computes RS codeword via FFT\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs:** `split_ext_to_base_col_major_matrix()` overhead (independent of m)\n- **Variable costs:** Scales with `f_height * D_EF = 2^(m - k_whir) * 4`\n  - `batch_expand_pad()` scales with codeword_height\n  - `batch_ntt()` scales with codeword_height = 2^(log_rs_domain_size - 1)\n\n#### C. **Merkle Tree Construction**\n\n**Lines:** 318-323\n\n```rust\nlet g_tree = MerkleTreeGpu::<F, HS::Digest>::new_with_hash::<HS::MerkleHash>(\n    DeviceMatrix::new(Arc::new(g_rs), codeword_height, D_EF),\n    1 << k_whir,\n    true,\n)\n```\n\n- Matrix dimensions: `codeword_height x D_EF` (width is always D_EF = 4)\n- Tree construction is width-independent; cost is proportional to total elements\n- Leaf size: `1 << k_whir` (k_whir is constant, default 4)\n\n#### D. **Batch Opening Queries**\n\n**Lines:** 358-464\n\nTwo different code paths depending on `whir_round`:\n\n**Round 0 (Lines 375-443):**\n- **Line 390:** `rs_code_matrix(log_blowup, layout, &traces, &d.inner.matrix)` — expensive operation that regenerates RS codewords if not cached\n  - This is called once per commitment in round 0\n  - Involves: batch_expand_pad, batch_ntt_small, mle_interpolate_stages, batch_ntt\n- **Lines 400-404:** `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on multiple trees (one per commitment)\n  - Queries all commitments' initial RS matrices\n- **Lines 419-424:** `batch_open_rows()` — retrieve actual row values for in-domain samples\n\n**Other rounds (Lines 444-464):**\n- **Lines 447-450:** `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` on single RS tree from previous round\n- **Lines 452-463:** `batch_open_rows()` on single tree, reconstitute from base field\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- **Fixed costs per query per round:**\n  - Merkle proof generation (independent of tree width)\n  - Row opening (depends on tree structure but not on matrix width much)\n- **Variable costs in round 0:**\n  - `rs_code_matrix()` cost is substantial and varies with total stacked width\n  - Cost depends on trace matrix widths being regenerated\n- **Variable costs in later rounds:**\n  - Only one codeword tree (width D_EF = 4) — essentially constant width\n  - Operations scale minimally with m\n\n### 4. **The `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round` Kernel**\n\n**CUDA File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu`\n**Lines:** 83-136 (kernel), 251-278 (launcher)\n\nThis kernel performs a single WHIR sumcheck round where:\n- Input: `f_coeffs` (MLE coefficients) and `w_moments` (superset sum moments)\n- Output: `s(1)` and `s(2)` — evaluations of the sumcheck polynomial at points 1 and 2\n\n**Kernel logic:**\n```cuda\nfor y in range(height / 2):\n    idx0 = 2*y\n    idx1 = 2*y + 1\n    \n    c0 = f_coeffs[idx0]\n    c1 = f_coeffs[idx1]\n    m0 = w_moments[idx0]\n    m1 = w_moments[idx1]\n    \n    // For X = 1:\n    f_1 = c0 + c1\n    term_1 = f_1 * m1\n    \n    // For X = 2:\n    f_2 = c0 + 2*c1\n    m_2 = 3*m1 - m0\n    term_2 = f_2 * m_2\n    \n    local_sums[0] += term_1\n    local_sums[1] += term_2\n```\n\n**Block reduction:** Uses `sumcheck::block_reduce_sum()` for parallel reduction\n\n**Cost characteristics:**\n- Kernel launches with `grid = (height / 2) / 256, block = 256`\n- Computation per thread: 2 FpExt multiplications and 2 additions\n- Followed by final reduction with `static_final_reduce_block_sums<S_DEG>` (line 274)\n- **Scaling:** O(height) = O(2^(m - round))\n\n### 5. **Fixed vs. Variable Costs Analysis**\n\n#### **Fixed Costs (Don't scale with trace width/APC):**\n\n1. **Per WHIR round (constant regardless of m):**\n   - PoW grinding for folding challenge (lines 243-247): `whir_params.folding_pow_bits` iterations\n   - PoW grinding for query phase (lines 360-364): `whir_params.query_phase_pow_bits` iterations\n   - Transcript sampling and observations: constant overhead\n   - Merkle tree root computation: fixed regardless of width\n   - Z-point computation (lines 477-495): O(num_queries * log_height) but log_height reduces each round\n\n2. **Per inner sumcheck round (across k_whir):**\n   - Transcript operations: constant\n   - Kernel launch overhead: fixed\n\n3. **Initial round only:**\n   - `rs_code_matrix()` regeneration cost if backing matrix not cached (line 390)\n   - But this scales with **total stacked width**, not per-commitment width change\n\n#### **Variable Costs (Scale with matrix dimensions/APC):**\n\n1. **Sumcheck kernel (lines 224-235):**\n   - O(2^(m - round)) — halves each inner round\n   - Total per WHIR round: O(2^(m) + 2^(m-1) + ... + 2^(m-k_whir)) ≈ O(2^m)\n\n2. **Coefficient/moment folding kernel (lines 254-268):**\n   - O(2^(m - round - 1)) — halves each inner round\n   - Total per WHIR round: similar to sumcheck\n\n3. **RS code expansion (lines 299-316):**\n   - `batch_expand_pad()`: O(codeword_height * D_EF)\n   - `batch_ntt()`: O(codeword_height * D_EF * log(codeword_height))\n   - Where codeword_height = 2^(log_rs_domain_size - 1)\n   - Scales with m but independent of **trace width**\n\n4. **Moment accumulation (lines 501-510):**\n   - `w_moments_accumulate()`: O(2^(m - k_whir))\n   - Called once per WHIR round (not per inner round)\n   - Scales with m\n\n### 6. **WHIR Round Count Comparison: APC 300 vs APC 0**\n\nFrom `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/config.rs` (lines 286-288):\n\n```rust\nlet num_rounds = log_stacked_height\n    .saturating_sub(whir_params.log_final_poly_len)\n    .div_ceil(k_whir);\n```\n\n**APC (Assumed to mean trace width/column count):**\n- **APC 300:** Larger trace width → larger stacked matrix width → larger m (log height)\n  - `m = log_stacked_height = l_skip + n_stack` (constant)\n  - Wait, m is actually **constant** regardless of trace width!\n  - Actually, m depends on: `log_stacked_height = log2(2^(l_skip + n_stack))`\n\n**Critical insight:** The variable `m` in the code is the **log of the stacked height**, not the width. Width only affects the **number of commitments and their sizes**, not the WHIR round structure itself.\n\nSo:\n- **WHIR rounds:** Always `(m - log_final_poly_len) / k_whir`\n- This is **constant** across different APC settings if using same l_skip, n_stack, k_whir\n- **BUT** each WHIR round does more work if there are more columns in the initial RS code matrix construction\n\n**The scaling difference comes from:**\n1. **Round 0:** `rs_code_matrix()` is called per commitment, and regeneration cost scales with **total trace width**\n2. **Later rounds:** Only D_EF=4 columns, so essentially constant\n\n### 7. **Why WHIR Scales 1.71x Instead of 2.35x**\n\nThe 1.71x scaling (vs expected 2.35x from width ratio) comes from:\n\n1. **Fixed costs dominate in early rounds:**\n   - PoW grinding overhead doesn't scale with width\n   - Merkle tree operations have significant fixed overhead\n   - Moment accumulation (line 501) uses GPU kernels with launch overhead\n\n2. **Round 0 dominates, but with diminishing returns:**\n   - `rs_code_matrix()` scales with trace width **only in round 0**\n   - In rounds 1+, only D_EF=4 columns are used\n   - So 80% of rounds have essentially constant-width work\n\n3. **Kernel launch overhead:**\n   - Each `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round()` launch has fixed GPU overhead\n   - Sumcheck computation is O(height), but height reduction per inner round limits gains\n\n4. **Moment accumulation cost:**\n   - `w_moments_accumulate()` kernel (lines 501-510) runs once per WHIR round\n   - Complexity: O(2^(m - k_whir)) which is **independent of trace width**\n   - This is a fixed bottleneck across APC settings\n\n### 8. **Key File References and Line Numbers**\n\n| Component | File | Lines | Purpose |\n|-----------|------|-------|---------|\n| Main WHIR prover | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 56-531 | GPU WHIR entry point |\n| WHIR loop | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 199-515 | Main WHIR round loop |\n| Sumcheck per round | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 202-271 | k_whir inner rounds |\n| RS expansion | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 289-316 | Code expansion & Merkle |\n| Batch queries | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 358-464 | Merkle proofs & openings |\n| Moment updates | `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs` | 472-510 | W-moment accumulation |\n| Sumcheck kernel | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 83-136 | Kernel implementation |\n| Kernel launcher | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 251-278 | Kernel dispatch |\n| Folding kernel | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 141-166 | Coefficient folding |\n| Moment accumulator | `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/whir.cu` | 196-221 | Moment kernel |\n| RS code matrix | `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` | 182-279 | RS code construction |\n| Config/rounds | `crates/stark-backend/src/config.rs` | 274-329 |