in Round 0; batching them into 2-4 descriptor-array kernel launches is expected to reduce Round 0 from 179ms to ~140-155ms, saving 25-40ms on STARK excl trace.","+MLE Rounds is the most anti-scaling STARK component: it takes 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0 (0.71x ratio), even though APC 300 has 2.35x fewer cells. The cause is per-round overhead in `fold_mle_evals`, which rebuilds and uploads 4 descriptor arrays (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_heights, widths) per fold call, twice per round (mat_evals + selectors), across 14 rounds — totaling 112 DeviceBuffer allocations + H2D uploads + frees per segment. Since fold buffer layouts are fully determined by initial trace heights/widths and the pingpong scheme, all descriptors can be pre-computed once and bulk-uploaded before the MLE loop, and the two per-round fold calls can be fused into a single kernel launch, eliminating per-round descriptor overhead and halving fold kernel launches."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-1800-reenable-batch-round0-small-airs\n\nRe-enable the batch Round 0 descriptor-array kernel path for small AIRs, which was previously implemented (commits 0d1bb2f9, bcb1c0d2) but reverted due to GPU OOM at APC 100/300. The OOM blocker has since been resolved by the VPMM page size increase (16 MiB) and PRUNE_SHMEM_KERNELS, as confirmed by the current successful APC 300 benchmark run (1106ms STARK excl trace). At APC 300, ~250 small coset-parallel AIRs per segment currently require ~1000 individual kernel launches in Round 0; batching them into 2-4 descriptor-array kernel launches is expected to reduce Round 0 from 179ms to ~140-155ms, saving 25-40ms on STARK excl trace.\n"}}
