{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017ju3GBUEJKex9Tq9Tm2nt9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-round descriptor overhead in MLE fold by pre-computing the complete fold buffer layout for all rounds and uploading it once before the MLE loop. Additionally, fuse the two per-round fold calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This targets the MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio APC 0/APC 300) caused by per-AIR overhead with 623 AIR instances at APC 300."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56bc24eb-d9a0-4ad2-8690-031f38c3d9d4","uuid":"2e580e9c-3e27-4d9f-b92d-221c4821bca8","timestamp":"2026-04-15T19:12:47.876Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-round descriptor overhead in MLE fold by pre-computing the complete fold buffer layout for all rounds and uploading it once before the MLE loop. Additionally, fuse the two per-round fold calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This targets the MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio APC 0/APC 300) caused by per-AIR overhead with 623 AIR instances at APC 300.","numLines":5,"startLine":1,"totalLines":270}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EcCPLknjzxkk8EnpvGYRtL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01743CFQgBnzvPQ5w1sFHX6F","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce per-round overhead in MLE fold by: (1) pre-computing fold descriptor arrays for all rounds and bulk-uploading once before the MLE loop, and (2) fusing the two per-round `fold_pingpong` calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This replaces 112 per-round `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment with 8 bulk uploads and 14 launches.\n\n**Savings estimate**: 5-15ms at APC 300 based on measured per-call overhead of ~0.3-0.5μs (from `bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers` report) and ~0.3ms inter-kernel gap per launch. The fold descriptor overhead is a small fraction of MLE Rounds (166ms), which is dominated by kernel execution time (~113ms). This optimization targets the remaining ~53ms overhead budget.\n\n**Why this is worth doing despite modest savings**: The MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio, 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0) is the single largest scaling bottleneck. Even a 10ms improvement compounds: it brings the APC 0/APC 300 ratio from 1.61x to 1.62x. More importantly, eliminating per-round fold overhead creates a cleaner baseline for future MLE round optimizations (which have been hard to isolate due to noise from this overhead).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Pingpong buffer allocation (mod.rs:622-647)\nAfter Round 0 completes, 4 `DeviceBuffer<EF>` are allocated:\n- `fold_mat_buf_a`, `fold_mat_buf_b` — pingpong pair for evaluation matrices\n- `fold_sel_buf_a`, `fold_sel_buf_b` — pingpong pair for selectors\n\n### MLE rounds loop (mod.rs:660-677)\nEach round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`.\n\n### fold_mle_evals (mod.rs:2051-2204)\nCalls `fold_pingpong` twice:\n1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (flattened `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)\n2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (`Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)\n\nBetween these, lines 2169-2177 update `memory_limit_bytes` when `save_memory` is true.\n\n### fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2058-2145)\nEach invocation:\n1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates foldable matrices to build 4 arrays: `input_ptrs`, `output_ptrs`, `log_heights`, `widths`.\n2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` — allocates DeviceBuffer (cudaMallocAsync) + copies.\n3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(...)`.\n4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`.\n5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into swapped buf_a.\n6. **Implicit cleanup**: 4 DeviceBuffers dropped (cudaFreeAsync).\n\n### Per-segment costs at APC 300\n- 14 rounds × 2 fold calls × (4 to_device + 1 kernel + 4 drops) = **112 to_device + 28 kernels + 112 drops = 252 CUDA API calls per segment**\n\n### CUDA kernel (sumcheck.cu:192-207, 304-318)\n`batch_fold_mle_kernel` uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index. Reads per-matrix pointers and dimensions from device arrays. Launcher uses `fold_mle_launch_params` for 2D grid sizing.\n\n### Pingpong buffer invariant\nAfter `fold_pingpong`, buf_a always contains the latest output. Physically:\n- Round R output goes to phys_B (the pre-swap buf_b), then swap makes buf_a = phys_B.\n- So input for round R+1 is always in the physical buffer that round R wrote to.\n- Parity: round R writes to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Pre-compute fold descriptor arrays (Rust)\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAfter the pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), add a pre-computation step:\n\n```rust\n// Pre-compute fold descriptors for all rounds.\n// For each round R in 1..=n_max and for each fold group (mat, sel):\n//   - Which matrices are foldable (height >> (R-1) > 1)\n//   - Input pointers (round 1: original matrix ptrs; round R>=2: offset in pingpong buf)\n//   - Output offsets in the alternate pingpong buffer\n//   - log_heights and widths\n\nstruct PrecomputedFold {\n    /// Flat buffer of input pointers for all rounds, indexed by round_offsets.\n    all_input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>,      // round 1: absolute ptrs; R>=2: cast from buf base + offset\n    all_output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>,\n    all_log_heights: Vec<u8>,\n    all_widths: Vec<u32>,\n    /// Per-round: (start_index, num_foldable, max_output_cells) for mat and sel groups.\n    round_meta: Vec<FoldRoundMeta>,\n    /// Physical base pointers captured at build time.\n    mat_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    mat_phys_b: *mut EF,\n    sel_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    sel_phys_b: *mut EF,\n}\n\nstruct FoldRoundMeta {\n    mat_start: usize,\n    mat_count: u16,\n    mat_max_cells: u32,\n    sel_start: usize,\n    sel_count: u16,\n    sel_max_cells: u32,\n}\n```\n\nThe builder iterates over rounds 1..=n_max. For each round:\n- Determines which matrices are foldable based on initial heights\n- For round 1: input_ptrs = absolute device pointers from current DeviceMatrix objects\n- For round R ≥ 2: input_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset matching previous round's output layout\n- Output_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset for this round's foldable set\n- The output offset for trace i at round R = `sum_{j<i, foldable at R} (width_j × (initial_height_j >> R))`\n\n**Critical invariant**: The output offsets at round R must match the input offsets at round R+1 exactly. Both use the same iteration order (sorted by height descending, matching the existing code's `partition_point` ordering).\n\nAfter building the CPU arrays, upload them in 4 bulk to_device() calls (one each for input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_heights, widths). These are indexed by round offset during the MLE loop.\n\n### Change 2: Fuse mat+sel fold into single kernel launch\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n\nNo new kernel needed. Instead, concatenate the mat and sel descriptor entries into the same arrays:\n- For round R: entries [0..mat_count) are mat_evals, entries [mat_count..mat_count+sel_count) are selectors\n- The existing `batch_fold_mle_kernel` processes all entries uniformly (it doesn't care about the semantic group)\n- Each entry has the correct input/output pointers pointing to the appropriate physical buffer\n\nThe key insight: the existing kernel already processes matrices by index (`blockIdx.y`). By concatenating mat and sel entries, one kernel launch handles both groups.\n\n### Change 3: Modify fold_mle_evals to use pre-computed arrays\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nReplace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with:\n\n```rust\nfn fold_mle_evals_precomputed(&mut self, round: usize, r_round: EF) -> Result<(), ...> {\n    let plan = self.fold_plan.as_ref().unwrap();\n    let meta = &plan.round_meta[round - 1];\n    let total = meta.mat_count as usize + meta.sel_count as usize;\n    if total == 0 { return Ok(()); }\n\n    let combined_start = meta.mat_start; // mat and sel are concatenated\n    let max_cells = max(meta.mat_max_cells, meta.sel_max_cells);\n\n    // Single kernel launch for both mat + sel\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &plan.d_all_input_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n            &plan.d_all_output_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n            &plan.d_all_widths.slice(combined_start, total),\n            total as u16,\n            &plan.d_all_log_heights.slice(combined_start, total),\n            max_cells,\n            r_round,\n        )?;\n    }\n\n    // Swap both buffer pairs (matches the fold_pingpong behavior)\n    if meta.mat_count > 0 {\n        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_mat_buf_a, &mut self.fold_mat_buf_b) {\n            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n        }\n    }\n    if meta.sel_count > 0 {\n        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_sel_buf_a, &mut self.fold_sel_buf_b) {\n            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Reconstruct views using pre-computed offsets (same logic as fold_pingpong lines 2122-2142)\n    // ... rebuild mat_evals_per_trace and sels_per_trace views from swapped buf_a\n    // using the pre-computed output offsets for this round\n\n    // save_memory update (same as lines 2169-2177)\n    if self.save_memory { ... }\n\n    // eq_xis trim + eq_ns update (same as lines 2193-2203)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n**Note on DeviceBuffer::slice**: The pre-uploaded arrays are indexed by offset. A `slice(start, len)` view into the DeviceBuffer provides the correct sub-range without new allocation. If `DeviceBuffer` doesn't support slicing, pass base pointer + offset as raw pointers.\n\n### Change 4: Fallback path\n\nKeep the existing `fold_pingpong` code for when `fold_plan.is_none()` (n_max = 0 or no foldable matrices).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Fold arithmetic**: `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` preserved exactly — same kernel, same inputs, same outputs.\n\n2. **Buffer pointer identity**: The pre-computed plan captures physical buffer pointers BEFORE any swaps. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but physical GPU addresses don't change. The plan's pointers always refer to physical addresses.\n\n3. **Offset thread-through**: Output offsets at round R define the data layout. Input offsets at round R+1 must match exactly. Both are computed from the same sorted iteration order (height descending) with the same foldability criteria.\n\n4. **Non-foldable view preservation**: Matrices that have reached height=1 are not in any round's descriptor entries. Their `DeviceMatrix` views remain unchanged across rounds, pointing to wherever they last landed (matching the existing `extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_foldable..])` behavior).\n\n5. **save_memory ordering**: The `memory_limit_bytes` update (lines 2169-2177) must see the NEW mat_evals views before computing sizes. In the fused approach, both mat and sel fold happen in one kernel, so the view reconstruction for mat_evals MUST precede the save_memory update. This matches the current sequencing (mat fold → save_memory → sel fold) because the save_memory update only reads mat_evals, not sel.\n\n6. **Proof correctness**: Only the fold parameter `r_round` changes per round. All pre-computed data depends on static trace metadata (initial heights, widths, pointer addresses), not on transcript challenges.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `run_pairing.sh` benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300}\n2. Compare with spec.py:\n   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, expect: 155-161ms)\n   - **Secondary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (current: 1119ms)\n   - **Regression check**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 (current: 1800ms, must not increase by >20ms)\n3. Verify: all configs prove + verify with `--recursion`\n4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in fold phase (14 per segment vs 28)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 3ms (below measurement noise floor, indicating zero real impact)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 20ms\n- Any proof verification failure\n- Any CUDA error\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1437,"cache_read_input_tokens":203958,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1437},"output_tokens":68,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56bc24eb-d9a0-4ad2-8690-031f38c3d9d4","uuid":"02cabef3-f99a-4533-bc1e-3209fd90a608"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01743CFQgBnzvPQ5w1sFHX6F","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56bc24eb-d9a0-4ad2-8690-031f38c3d9d4","uuid":"cf33da77-3426-4231-84ad-5c4fe7c63a8b","timestamp":"2026-04-15T19:14:03.572Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-15-2100-precompute-mle-fold-layout/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce per-round overhead in MLE fold by: (1) pre-computing fold descriptor arrays for all rounds and bulk-uploading once before the MLE loop, and (2) fusing the two per-round `fold_pingpong` calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This replaces 112 per-round `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment with 8 bulk uploads and 14 launches.\n\n**Savings estimate**: 5-15ms at APC 300 based on measured per-call overhead of ~0.3-0.5μs (from `bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers` report) and ~0.3ms inter-kernel gap per launch. The fold descriptor overhead is a small fraction of MLE Rounds (166ms), which is dominated by kernel execution time (~113ms). This optimization targets the remaining ~53ms overhead budget.\n\n**Why this is worth doing despite modest savings**: The MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio, 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0) is the single largest scaling bottleneck. Even a 10ms improvement compounds: it brings the APC 0/APC 300 ratio from 1.61x to 1.62x. More importantly, eliminating per-round fold overhead creates a cleaner baseline for future MLE round optimizations (which have been hard to isolate due to noise from this overhead).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Pingpong buffer allocation (mod.rs:622-647)\nAfter Round 0 completes, 4 `DeviceBuffer<EF>` are allocated:\n- `fold_mat_buf_a`, `fold_mat_buf_b` — pingpong pair for evaluation matrices\n- `fold_sel_buf_a`, `fold_sel_buf_b` — pingpong pair for selectors\n\n### MLE rounds loop (mod.rs:660-677)\nEach round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`.\n\n### fold_mle_evals (mod.rs:2051-2204)\nCalls `fold_pingpong` twice:\n1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (flattened `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)\n2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (`Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)\n\nBetween these, lines 2169-2177 update `memory_limit_bytes` when `save_memory` is true.\n\n### fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2058-2145)\nEach invocation:\n1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates foldable matrices to build 4 arrays: `input_ptrs`, `output_ptrs`, `log_heights`, `widths`.\n2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` — allocates DeviceBuffer (cudaMallocAsync) + copies.\n3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(...)`.\n4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`.\n5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into swapped buf_a.\n6. **Implicit cleanup**: 4 DeviceBuffers dropped (cudaFreeAsync).\n\n### Per-segment costs at APC 300\n- 14 rounds × 2 fold calls × (4 to_device + 1 kernel + 4 drops) = **112 to_device + 28 kernels + 112 drops = 252 CUDA API calls per segment**\n\n### CUDA kernel (sumcheck.cu:192-207, 304-318)\n`batch_fold_mle_kernel` uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index. Reads per-matrix pointers and dimensions from device arrays. Launcher uses `fold_mle_launch_params` for 2D grid sizing.\n\n### Pingpong buffer invariant\nAfter `fold_pingpong`, buf_a always contains the latest output. Physically:\n- Round R output goes to phys_B (the pre-swap buf_b), then swap makes buf_a = phys_B.\n- So input for round R+1 is always in the physical buffer that round R wrote to.\n- Parity: round R writes to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Pre-compute fold descriptor arrays (Rust)\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAfter the pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), add a pre-computation step:\n\n```rust\n// Pre-compute fold descriptors for all rounds.\n// For each round R in 1..=n_max and for each fold group (mat, sel):\n//   - Which matrices are foldable (height >> (R-1) > 1)\n//   - Input pointers (round 1: original matrix ptrs; round R>=2: offset in pingpong buf)\n//   - Output offsets in the alternate pingpong buffer\n//   - log_heights and widths\n\nstruct PrecomputedFold {\n    /// Flat buffer of input pointers for all rounds, indexed by round_offsets.\n    all_input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>,      // round 1: absolute ptrs; R>=2: cast from buf base + offset\n    all_output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>,\n    all_log_heights: Vec<u8>,\n    all_widths: Vec<u32>,\n    /// Per-round: (start_index, num_foldable, max_output_cells) for mat and sel groups.\n    round_meta: Vec<FoldRoundMeta>,\n    /// Physical base pointers captured at build time.\n    mat_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    mat_phys_b: *mut EF,\n    sel_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    sel_phys_b: *mut EF,\n}\n\nstruct FoldRoundMeta {\n    mat_start: usize,\n    mat_count: u16,\n    mat_max_cells: u32,\n    sel_start: usize,\n    sel_count: u16,\n    sel_max_cells: u32,\n}\n```\n\nThe builder iterates over rounds 1..=n_max. For each round:\n- Determines which matrices are foldable based on initial heights\n- For round 1: input_ptrs = absolute device pointers from current DeviceMatrix objects\n- For round R ≥ 2: input_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset matching previous round's output layout\n- Output_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset for this round's foldable set\n- The output offset for trace i at round R = `sum_{j<i, foldable at R} (width_j × (initial_height_j >> R))`\n\n**Critical invariant**: The output offsets at round R must match the input offsets at round R+1 exactly. Both use the same iteration order (sorted by height descending, matching the existing code's `partition_point` ordering).\n\nAfter building the CPU arrays, upload them in 4 bulk to_device() calls (one each for input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_heights, widths). These are indexed by round offset during the MLE loop.\n\n### Change 2: Fuse mat+sel fold into single kernel launch\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n\nNo new kernel needed. Instead, concatenate the mat and sel descriptor entries into the same arrays:\n- For round R: entries [0..mat_count) are mat_evals, entries [mat_count..mat_count+sel_count) are selectors\n- The existing `batch_fold_mle_kernel` processes all entries uniformly (it doesn't care about the semantic group)\n- Each entry has the correct input/output pointers pointing to the appropriate physical buffer\n\nThe key insight: the existing kernel already processes matrices by index (`blockIdx.y`). By concatenating mat and sel entries, one kernel launch handles both groups.\n\n### Change 3: Modify fold_mle_evals to use pre-computed arrays\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nReplace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with:\n\n```rust\nfn fold_mle_evals_precomputed(&mut self, round: usize, r_round: EF) -> Result<(), ...> {\n    let plan = self.fold_plan.as_ref().unwrap();\n    let meta = &plan.round_meta[round - 1];\n    let total = meta.mat_count as usize + meta.sel_count as usize;\n    if total == 0 { return Ok(()); }\n\n    let combined_start = meta.mat_start; // mat and sel are concatenated\n    let max_cells = max(meta.mat_max_cells, meta.sel_max_cells);\n\n    // Single kernel launch for both mat + sel\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &plan.d_all_input_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n            &plan.d_all_output_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n            &plan.d_all_widths.slice(combined_start, total),\n            total as u16,\n            &plan.d_all_log_heights.slice(combined_start, total),\n            max_cells,\n            r_round,\n        )?;\n    }\n\n    // Swap both buffer pairs (matches the fold_pingpong behavior)\n    if meta.mat_count > 0 {\n        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_mat_buf_a, &mut self.fold_mat_buf_b) {\n            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n        }\n    }\n    if meta.sel_count > 0 {\n        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_sel_buf_a, &mut self.fold_sel_buf_b) {\n            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Reconstruct views using pre-computed offsets (same logic as fold_pingpong lines 2122-2142)\n    // ... rebuild mat_evals_per_trace and sels_per_trace views from swapped buf_a\n    // using the pre-computed output offsets for this round\n\n    // save_memory update (same as lines 2169-2177)\n    if self.save_memory { ... }\n\n    // eq_xis trim + eq_ns update (same as lines 2193-2203)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n**Note on DeviceBuffer::slice**: The pre-uploaded arrays are indexed by offset. A `slice(start, len)` view into the DeviceBuffer provides the correct sub-range without new allocation. If `DeviceBuffer` doesn't support slicing, pass base pointer + offset as raw pointers.\n\n### Change 4: Fallback path\n\nKeep the existing `fold_pingpong` code for when `fold_plan.is_none()` (n_max = 0 or no foldable matrices).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Fold arithmetic**: `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` preserved exactly — same kernel, same inputs, same outputs.\n\n2. **Buffer pointer identity**: The pre-computed plan captures physical buffer pointers BEFORE any swaps. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but physical GPU addresses don't change. The plan's pointers always refer to physical addresses.\n\n3. **Offset thread-through**: Output offsets at round R define the data layout. Input offsets at round R+1 must match exactly. Both are computed from the same sorted iteration order (height descending) with the same foldability criteria.\n\n4. **Non-foldable view preservation**: Matrices that have reached height=1 are not in any round's descriptor entries. Their `DeviceMatrix` views remain unchanged across rounds, pointing to wherever they last landed (matching the existing `extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_foldable..])` behavior).\n\n5. **save_memory ordering**: The `memory_limit_bytes` update (lines 2169-2177) must see the NEW mat_evals views before computing sizes. In the fused approach, both mat and sel fold happen in one kernel, so the view reconstruction for mat_evals MUST precede the save_memory update. This matches the current sequencing (mat fold → save_memory → sel fold) because the save_memory update only reads mat_evals, not sel.\n\n6. **Proof correctness**: Only the fold parameter `r_round` changes per round. All pre-computed data depends on static trace metadata (initial heights, widths, pointer addresses), not on transcript challenges.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `run_pairing.sh` benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300}\n2. Compare with spec.py:\n   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, expect: 155-161ms)\n   - **Secondary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (current: 1119ms)\n   - **Regression check**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 (current: 1800ms, must not increase by >20ms)\n3. Verify: all configs prove + verify with `--recursion`\n4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in fold phase (14 per segment vs 28)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 3ms (below measurement noise floor, indicating zero real impact)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 20ms\n- Any proof verification failure\n- Any CUDA error\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":268,"newStart":2,"newLines":199,"lines":[" "," ## Goal"," ","-Eliminate per-round descriptor overhead in MLE fold by pre-computing the complete fold buffer layout for all rounds and uploading it once before the MLE loop. Additionally, fuse the two per-round fold calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This targets the MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio APC 0/APC 300) caused by per-AIR overhead with 623 AIR instances at APC 300.","+Reduce per-round overhead in MLE fold by: (1) pre-computing fold descriptor arrays for all rounds and bulk-uploading once before the MLE loop, and (2) fusing the two per-round `fold_pingpong` calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This replaces 112 per-round `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment with 8 bulk uploads and 14 launches."," ","+**Savings estimate**: 5-15ms at APC 300 based on measured per-call overhead of ~0.3-0.5μs (from `bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers` report) and ~0.3ms inter-kernel gap per launch. The fold descriptor overhead is a small fraction of MLE Rounds (166ms), which is dominated by kernel execution time (~113ms). This optimization targets the remaining ~53ms overhead budget.","+","+**Why this is worth doing despite modest savings**: The MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio, 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0) is the single largest scaling bottleneck. Even a 10ms improvement compounds: it brings the APC 0/APC 300 ratio from 1.61x to 1.62x. More importantly, eliminating per-round fold overhead creates a cleaner baseline for future MLE round optimizations (which have been hard to isolate due to noise from this overhead).","+"," ## Current Code Path"," ","-### Entry point","-`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 660-677: MLE rounds loop iterates `for round in 1..=n_max`.","+### Pingpong buffer allocation (mod.rs:622-647)","+After Round 0 completes, 4 `DeviceBuffer<EF>` are allocated:","+- `fold_mat_buf_a`, `fold_mat_buf_b` — pingpong pair for evaluation matrices","+- `fold_sel_buf_a`, `fold_sel_buf_b` — pingpong pair for selectors"," ","-### fold_mle_evals (lines 2051-2204)","-Each round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`, which calls the inner `fold_pingpong` function TWICE:","-1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (evaluation matrices, flattened from `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)","-2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (selector matrices, `Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)","+### MLE rounds loop (mod.rs:660-677)","+Each round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`."," ","-### fold_pingpong (lines 2058-2145)","+### fold_mle_evals (mod.rs:2051-2204)","+Calls `fold_pingpong` twice:","+1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (flattened `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)","+2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (`Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)","+","+Between these, lines 2169-2177 update `memory_limit_bytes` when `save_memory` is true.","+","+### fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2058-2145)"," Each invocation:","-1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates over all foldable matrices (those with height > 1) to collect 4 arrays:","-   - `input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>` — current matrix data pointers","-   - `output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>` — offsets into the alternate pingpong buffer (buf_b)","-   - `log_heights: Vec<u8>` — log2(output height)","-   - `widths: Vec<u32>` — matrix widths","-2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` calls, each allocating a DeviceBuffer via `cudaMallocAsync`, copying via `cudaMemcpyAsync`","-3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(d_input_ptrs, d_output_ptrs, d_widths, num_foldable, d_log_heights, max_output_cells, r_round)`","-4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`","-5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Creates non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into the swapped buf_a","-6. **Implicit free**: 4 DeviceBuffers from step 2 are dropped (cudaFreeAsync)","+1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates foldable matrices to build 4 arrays: `input_ptrs`, `output_ptrs`, `log_heights`, `widths`.","+2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` — allocates DeviceBuffer (cudaMallocAsync) + copies.","+3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(...)`.","+4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`.","+5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into swapped buf_a.","+6. **Implicit cleanup**: 4 DeviceBuffers dropped (cudaFreeAsync)."," ","-### CUDA kernel","-`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`, lines 192-207: `batch_fold_mle_kernel` — uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index, reads per-matrix input/output pointers and dimensions from device arrays.","+### Per-segment costs at APC 300","+- 14 rounds × 2 fold calls × (4 to_device + 1 kernel + 4 drops) = **112 to_device + 28 kernels + 112 drops = 252 CUDA API calls per segment**"," ","-### Per-round cost at APC 300","-- 2 fold calls × (4 CPU Vec iterations over ~300 entries + 4 cudaMallocAsync + 4 cudaMemcpyAsync + 1 kernel launch + 4 cudaFreeAsync) = 8 Vec iterations + 8 alloc + 8 memcpy + 2 kernels + 8 free","-- 14 rounds × above = **112 Vec iterations + 112 cudaMallocAsync + 112 cudaMemcpyAsync + 28 kernel launches + 112 cudaFreeAsync = 364 CUDA API calls + 112 CPU collection loops per segment**","-- Plus 28 DeviceMatrix view reconstruction passes per segment","+### CUDA kernel (sumcheck.cu:192-207, 304-318)","+`batch_fold_mle_kernel` uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index. Reads per-matrix pointers and dimensions from device arrays. Launcher uses `fold_mle_launch_params` for 2D grid sizing."," "," ### Pingpong buffer invariant","-After `fold_pingpong`, `buf_a` always contains the latest fold output:","-- Round 1: input = original matrix pointers, output → buf_b, swap → buf_a has output","-- Round R (R ≥ 2): input = buf_a, output → buf_b, swap → buf_a has output","+After `fold_pingpong`, buf_a always contains the latest output. Physically:","+- Round R output goes to phys_B (the pre-swap buf_b), then swap makes buf_a = phys_B.","+- So input for round R+1 is always in the physical buffer that round R wrote to.","+- Parity: round R writes to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}."," ","-In terms of physical buffers (let phys_A, phys_B be the original allocations):","-- Round 1 (odd): write to phys_B, swap → logical buf_a = phys_B","-- Round 2 (even): write to phys_A, swap → logical buf_a = phys_A","-- Round R: write to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}, swap → logical buf_a = phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}","-"," ## Changes"," ","-### Change 1: New `FoldPlan` struct and builder (Rust side)","+### Change 1: Pre-compute fold descriptor arrays (Rust)"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (or a new `fold_plan.rs` module)","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`"," ","-Add:","+After the pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), add a pre-computation step:","+"," ```rust","-/// Per-matrix descriptor for the pre-computed fold layout.","-/// Stored contiguously for all rounds: round 1 mat_evals, round 1 sels, round 2 mat_evals, ...","-#[repr(C)]","-struct FoldEntry {","-    /// Round 1: absolute device pointer to original matrix data.","-    /// Round R >= 2: byte offset from the appropriate physical buffer base.","-    input_ptr_or_offset: u64,","-    /// Byte offset from the output physical buffer base for this round's output.","-    output_offset: u32,","-    /// log2(output_height) for this entry at this round.","-    log_output_height: u8,","-    /// Matrix width (constant across rounds).","-    width: u32,","-    /// 0 = mat_eval, 1 = selector (determines which buffer pair to use).","-    group: u8,","-}","+// Pre-compute fold descriptors for all rounds.","+// For each round R in 1..=n_max and for each fold group (mat, sel):","+//   - Which matrices are foldable (height >> (R-1) > 1)","+//   - Input pointers (round 1: original matrix ptrs; round R>=2: offset in pingpong buf)","+//   - Output offsets in the alternate pingpong buffer","+//   - log_heights and widths"," ","-/// Per-round metadata.","-#[repr(C)]","-struct FoldRoundInfo {","-    /// Offset into the FoldEntry array where this round's entries start.","-    entry_offset: u32,","-    /// Number of foldable entries this round (mat + sel combined).","-    num_entries: u32,","-    /// Maximum output cells across all entries (for grid sizing).","-    max_output_cells: u32,","-}","-","-struct FoldPlan {","-    /// All entries for all rounds, uploaded once.","-    d_entries: DeviceBuffer<FoldEntry>,","-    /// Per-round info, uploaded once.","-    d_round_info: DeviceBuffer<FoldRoundInfo>,","-    /// Physical buffer base pointers for mat_evals (A and B).","+struct PrecomputedFold {","+    /// Flat buffer of input pointers for all rounds, indexed by round_offsets.","+    all_input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>,      // round 1: absolute ptrs; R>=2: cast from buf base + offset","+    all_output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>,","+    all_log_heights: Vec<u8>,","+    all_widths: Vec<u32>,","+    /// Per-round: (start_index, num_foldable, max_output_cells) for mat and sel groups.","+    round_meta: Vec<FoldRoundMeta>,","+    /// Physical base pointers captured at build time.","     mat_phys_a: *mut EF,","     mat_phys_b: *mut EF,","-    /// Physical buffer base pointers for selectors (A and B).","     sel_phys_a: *mut EF,","     sel_phys_b: *mut EF,","-    /// Number of rounds.","-    n_max: usize,"," }","+","+struct FoldRoundMeta {","+    mat_start: usize,","+    mat_count: u16,","+    mat_max_cells: u32,","+    sel_start: usize,","+    sel_count: u16,","+    sel_max_cells: u32,","+}"," ```"," ","-**Builder function** `FoldPlan::build(mat_evals_per_trace, sels_per_trace, n_max, mat_buf_a, mat_buf_b, sel_buf_a, sel_buf_b)`:","-1. Record physical buffer base pointers from buf_a and buf_b BEFORE any swaps.","-2. For each round R in 1..=n_max:","-   a. For mat_evals (flattened): iterate over matrices, filter those with `initial_height >> (R-1) > 1` (foldable). For each foldable matrix:","-      - Round 1: `input_ptr_or_offset` = matrix's current device pointer (absolute)","-      - Round R≥2: `input_ptr_or_offset` = byte offset of this matrix's data within the physical buffer that is the input for round R (= previous round's output buffer)","-      - `output_offset` = cumulative byte offset in the output physical buffer","-      - `log_output_height` = log2(initial_height >> R)","-      - `width` = matrix width","-      - `group` = 0","-   b. For selectors: same as above with `group` = 1 and selector buffer pointers","-   c. Store `FoldRoundInfo` for this round","-3. Upload `d_entries` and `d_round_info` to device (2 H2D transfers total, replacing 112).","+The builder iterates over rounds 1..=n_max. For each round:","+- Determines which matrices are foldable based on initial heights","+- For round 1: input_ptrs = absolute device pointers from current DeviceMatrix objects","+- For round R ≥ 2: input_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset matching previous round's output layout","+- Output_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset for this round's foldable set","+- The output offset for trace i at round R = `sum_{j<i, foldable at R} (width_j × (initial_height_j >> R))`"," ","-The output offset calculation for round R, entry i:","-```","-offset[R][i] = sum_{j < i, j foldable at round R} (width_j × (height_j >> R))","-```","-This depends only on initial heights and widths (known at plan build time).","+**Critical invariant**: The output offsets at round R must match the input offsets at round R+1 exactly. Both use the same iteration order (sorted by height descending, matching the existing code's `partition_point` ordering)."," ","-The input offset for round R ≥ 2 equals the output offset from round R-1 (since the previous output becomes the current input). For round 1, input is absolute pointers.","+After building the CPU arrays, upload them in 4 bulk to_device() calls (one each for input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_heights, widths). These are indexed by round offset during the MLE loop."," ","-### Change 2: New fused fold CUDA kernel","+### Change 2: Fuse mat+sel fold into single kernel launch"," "," **File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`"," ","-Add a new kernel:","-```cuda","-struct FoldEntry {","-    uint64_t input_ptr_or_offset;","-    uint32_t output_offset;","-    uint8_t log_output_height;","-    uint32_t width;","-    uint8_t group; // 0 = mat, 1 = sel","-};","+No new kernel needed. Instead, concatenate the mat and sel descriptor entries into the same arrays:","+- For round R: entries [0..mat_count) are mat_evals, entries [mat_count..mat_count+sel_count) are selectors","+- The existing `batch_fold_mle_kernel` processes all entries uniformly (it doesn't care about the semantic group)","+- Each entry has the correct input/output pointers pointing to the appropriate physical buffer"," ","-struct FoldRoundInfo {","-    uint32_t entry_offset;","-    uint32_t num_entries;","-    uint32_t max_output_cells;","-};","+The key insight: the existing kernel already processes matrices by index (`blockIdx.y`). By concatenating mat and sel entries, one kernel launch handles both groups."," ","-__global__ void batch_fold_mle_planned_kernel(","-    const FoldEntry *entries,","-    const FoldRoundInfo *round_info,","-    const FpExt *mat_input_base,   // physical buffer that is INPUT for this round (mat)","-    FpExt *mat_output_base,        // physical buffer that is OUTPUT for this round (mat)","-    const FpExt *sel_input_base,   // same for selectors","-    FpExt *sel_output_base,","-    uint32_t round,                // 1-indexed round number","-    FpExt r_val","-) {","-    FoldRoundInfo ri = round_info[round - 1];","-    uint32_t mat_idx = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;","-    if (mat_idx >= ri.num_entries) return;","+### Change 3: Modify fold_mle_evals to use pre-computed arrays"," ","-    FoldEntry e = entries[ri.entry_offset + mat_idx];","+**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`"," ","-    // Determine input and output pointers","-    const FpExt *input;","-    FpExt *output;","-    if (e.group == 0) {","-        input = (round == 1)","-            ? reinterpret_cast<const FpExt *>(e.input_ptr_or_offset)","-            : mat_input_base + (e.input_ptr_or_offset / sizeof(FpExt));","-        output = mat_output_base + (e.output_offset / sizeof(FpExt));","-    } else {","-        input = (round == 1)","-            ? reinterpret_cast<const FpExt *>(e.input_ptr_or_offset)","-            : sel_input_base + (e.input_ptr_or_offset / sizeof(FpExt));","-        output = sel_output_base + (e.output_offset / sizeof(FpExt));","-    }","+Replace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with:"," ","-    uint32_t tidx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;","-    // Standard fold: output[j] = input[j] + r * (input[j + half_h * w] - input[j])","-    sumcheck::fold_mle_from_ptrs(input, output, e.width, e.log_output_height, r_val, tidx);","-}","-```","+```rust","+fn fold_mle_evals_precomputed(&mut self, round: usize, r_round: EF) -> Result<(), ...> {","+    let plan = self.fold_plan.as_ref().unwrap();","+    let meta = &plan.round_meta[round - 1];","+    let total = meta.mat_count as usize + meta.sel_count as usize;","+    if total == 0 { return Ok(()); }"," ","-A thin helper `fold_mle_from_ptrs` extracts the fold logic from the existing `sumcheck::fold_mle` to accept direct pointers instead of pointer-to-pointer arrays.","+    let combined_start = meta.mat_start; // mat and sel are concatenated","+    let max_cells = max(meta.mat_max_cells, meta.sel_max_cells);"," ","-**Launcher:**","-```cuda","-extern \"C\" int _batch_fold_mle_planned(","-    const FoldEntry *entries,","-    const FoldRoundInfo *round_info,","-    const FpExt *mat_input_base,","-    FpExt *mat_output_base,","-    const FpExt *sel_input_base,","-    FpExt *sel_output_base,","-    uint32_t round,","-    uint32_t max_output_cells,","-    uint32_t num_entries,","-    FpExt r_val","-) {","-    auto [grid, block] = fold_mle_launch_params(max_output_cells, num_entries);","-    batch_fold_mle_planned_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(","-        entries, round_info,","-        mat_input_base, mat_output_base,","-        sel_input_base, sel_output_base,","-        round, r_val","-    );","-    return CHECK_KERNEL();","-}","-```","+    // Single kernel launch for both mat + sel","+    unsafe {","+        batch_fold_mle(","+            &plan.d_all_input_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),","+            &plan.d_all_output_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),","+            &plan.d_all_widths.slice(combined_start, total),","+            total as u16,","+            &plan.d_all_log_heights.slice(combined_start, total),","+            max_cells,","+            r_round,","+        )?;","+    }"," ","-### Change 3: FFI binding","+    // Swap both buffer pairs (matches the fold_pingpong behavior)","+    if meta.mat_count > 0 {","+        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_mat_buf_a, &mut self.fold_mat_buf_b) {","+            std::mem::swap(a, b);","+        }","+    }","+    if meta.sel_count > 0 {","+        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_sel_buf_a, &mut self.fold_sel_buf_b) {","+            std::mem::swap(a, b);","+        }","+    }"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/mod.rs`","+    // Reconstruct views using pre-computed offsets (same logic as fold_pingpong lines 2122-2142)","+    // ... rebuild mat_evals_per_trace and sels_per_trace views from swapped buf_a","+    // using the pre-computed output offsets for this round"," ","-Add extern declaration and safe wrapper for `_batch_fold_mle_planned`. The wrapper takes `&FoldPlan`, `round: u32`, `r_val: EF` and computes the correct physical buffer pair based on round parity:","-- Round 1 (odd): mat output = mat_phys_b, sel output = sel_phys_b","-- Round R even: mat input = mat_phys_b, mat output = mat_phys_a (and symmetric for sel)","-- Round R odd (R > 1): mat input = mat_phys_a, mat output = mat_phys_b","+    // save_memory update (same as lines 2169-2177)","+    if self.save_memory { ... }"," ","-### Change 4: Modify fold_mle_evals to use FoldPlan","+    // eq_xis trim + eq_ns update (same as lines 2193-2203)","+    ...","+}","+```"," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`","+**Note on DeviceBuffer::slice**: The pre-uploaded arrays are indexed by offset. A `slice(start, len)` view into the DeviceBuffer provides the correct sub-range without new allocation. If `DeviceBuffer` doesn't support slicing, pass base pointer + offset as raw pointers."," ","-1. Add `fold_plan: Option<FoldPlan>` field to `LogupZerocheckGpu`.","-2. After pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), build the FoldPlan:","-   ```rust","-   prover.fold_plan = Some(FoldPlan::build(","-       &prover.mat_evals_per_trace,","-       &prover.sels_per_trace,","-       n_max,","-       &prover.fold_mat_buf_a, &prover.fold_mat_buf_b,","-       &prover.fold_sel_buf_a, &prover.fold_sel_buf_b,","-   )?);","-   ```","-3. In `fold_mle_evals`, replace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with a single `batch_fold_mle_planned` call using the pre-built plan.","-4. After the kernel, swap both buffer pairs: `swap(mat_buf_a, mat_buf_b)` and `swap(sel_buf_a, sel_buf_b)`.","-5. Update the `DeviceMatrix` views in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` to point into the swapped buf_a using pre-computed offsets from the plan.","+### Change 4: Fallback path"," ","-### Change 5: Keep the old fold_pingpong as fallback","+Keep the existing `fold_pingpong` code for when `fold_plan.is_none()` (n_max = 0 or no foldable matrices)."," ","-**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`","-","-Retain the existing `fold_pingpong` for the case where `fold_plan.is_none()` (e.g., when n_max is 0 or total cells are 0). The plan is used only when both mat and sel buffers are allocated and n_max > 0.","-"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Fold correctness**: For each matrix at each round, `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` must be preserved exactly. The planned kernel computes the same operation with pointers derived from the layout table instead of per-round host arrays.","+1. **Fold arithmetic**: `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` preserved exactly — same kernel, same inputs, same outputs."," ","-2. **Buffer ownership**: After fold_mle_evals, `mat_evals_per_trace` views must point to valid data in the current `buf_a`. The plan's pre-computed offsets must match the actual buffer layout at each round.","+2. **Buffer pointer identity**: The pre-computed plan captures physical buffer pointers BEFORE any swaps. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but physical GPU addresses don't change. The plan's pointers always refer to physical addresses."," ","-3. **Non-foldable matrix views**: Matrices that have reached height=1 keep their existing views (pointing into whichever buffer they last landed in). The planned kernel skips them (they're not in the fold entries for that round). The Rust-side view update must also skip them.","+3. **Offset thread-through**: Output offsets at round R define the data layout. Input offsets at round R+1 must match exactly. Both are computed from the same sorted iteration order (height descending) with the same foldability criteria."," ","-4. **Physical buffer identity**: The plan stores the raw physical buffer pointers captured at build time. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps the `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but does NOT change the physical GPU addresses. The kernel uses the physical addresses directly, so swaps are invisible to it — the round number determines which physical buffer is input/output.","+4. **Non-foldable view preservation**: Matrices that have reached height=1 are not in any round's descriptor entries. Their `DeviceMatrix` views remain unchanged across rounds, pointing to wherever they last landed (matching the existing `extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_foldable..])` behavior)."," ","-5. **Proof correctness**: The fold parameter `r_round` comes from the Fiat-Shamir transcript and is passed as a kernel argument each round. No pre-computation of `r_round` is attempted.","+5. **save_memory ordering**: The `memory_limit_bytes` update (lines 2169-2177) must see the NEW mat_evals views before computing sizes. In the fused approach, both mat and sel fold happen in one kernel, so the view reconstruction for mat_evals MUST precede the save_memory update. This matches the current sequencing (mat fold → save_memory → sel fold) because the save_memory update only reads mat_evals, not sel."," ","-6. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, with ~20 AIRs per segment, the plan build cost (~20 entries × 14 rounds) is negligible. The plan is still beneficial (eliminates 28 kernel launches) but the absolute savings are smaller.","+6. **Proof correctness**: Only the fold parameter `r_round` changes per round. All pre-computed data depends on static trace metadata (initial heights, widths, pointer addresses), not on transcript challenges."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-1. Run `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}","+1. Run `run_pairing.sh` benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300}"," 2. Compare with spec.py:","-   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, target: <150ms)","+   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, expect: 155-161ms)","    - **Secondary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (current: 1119ms)","    - **Regression check**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 (current: 1800ms, must not increase by >20ms)","-3. Verify proof correctness: all configs must prove + verify successfully with `--recursion`","-4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in MLE rounds (expect ~14 fold launches per segment instead of ~28)","+3. Verify: all configs prove + verify with `--recursion`","+4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in fold phase (14 per segment vs 28)"," "," ## Rollback Criteria"," ","-- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 10ms (below measurement noise)","+- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 3ms (below measurement noise floor, indicating zero real impact)"," - STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 20ms"," - Any proof verification failure","-- Any CUDA error (cudaErrorIllegalAddress, OOM)","+- Any CUDA error"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-round descriptor overhead in MLE fold by pre-computing the complete fold buffer layout for all rounds and uploading it once before the MLE loop. Additionally, fuse the two per-round fold calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This targets the MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio APC 0/APC 300) caused by per-AIR overhead with 623 AIR instances at APC 300.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 660-677: MLE rounds loop iterates `for round in 1..=n_max`.\n\n### fold_mle_evals (lines 2051-2204)\nEach round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`, which calls the inner `fold_pingpong` function TWICE:\n1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (evaluation matrices, flattened from `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)\n2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (selector matrices, `Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)\n\n### fold_pingpong (lines 2058-2145)\nEach invocation:\n1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates over all foldable matrices (those with height > 1) to collect 4 arrays:\n   - `input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>` — current matrix data pointers\n   - `output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>` — offsets into the alternate pingpong buffer (buf_b)\n   - `log_heights: Vec<u8>` — log2(output height)\n   - `widths: Vec<u32>` — matrix widths\n2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` calls, each allocating a DeviceBuffer via `cudaMallocAsync`, copying via `cudaMemcpyAsync`\n3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(d_input_ptrs, d_output_ptrs, d_widths, num_foldable, d_log_heights, max_output_cells, r_round)`\n4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`\n5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Creates non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into the swapped buf_a\n6. **Implicit free**: 4 DeviceBuffers from step 2 are dropped (cudaFreeAsync)\n\n### CUDA kernel\n`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`, lines 192-207: `batch_fold_mle_kernel` — uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index, reads per-matrix input/output pointers and dimensions from device arrays.\n\n### Per-round cost at APC 300\n- 2 fold calls × (4 CPU Vec iterations over ~300 entries + 4 cudaMallocAsync + 4 cudaMemcpyAsync + 1 kernel launch + 4 cudaFreeAsync) = 8 Vec iterations + 8 alloc + 8 memcpy + 2 kernels + 8 free\n- 14 rounds × above = **112 Vec iterations + 112 cudaMallocAsync + 112 cudaMemcpyAsync + 28 kernel launches + 112 cudaFreeAsync = 364 CUDA API calls + 112 CPU collection loops per segment**\n- Plus 28 DeviceMatrix view reconstruction passes per segment\n\n### Pingpong buffer invariant\nAfter `fold_pingpong`, `buf_a` always contains the latest fold output:\n- Round 1: input = original matrix pointers, output → buf_b, swap → buf_a has output\n- Round R (R ≥ 2): input = buf_a, output → buf_b, swap → buf_a has output\n\nIn terms of physical buffers (let phys_A, phys_B be the original allocations):\n- Round 1 (odd): write to phys_B, swap → logical buf_a = phys_B\n- Round 2 (even): write to phys_A, swap → logical buf_a = phys_A\n- Round R: write to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}, swap → logical buf_a = phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New `FoldPlan` struct and builder (Rust side)\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` (or a new `fold_plan.rs` module)\n\nAdd:\n```rust\n/// Per-matrix descriptor for the pre-computed fold layout.\n/// Stored contiguously for all rounds: round 1 mat_evals, round 1 sels, round 2 mat_evals, ...\n#[repr(C)]\nstruct FoldEntry {\n    /// Round 1: absolute device pointer to original matrix data.\n    /// Round R >= 2: byte offset from the appropriate physical buffer base.\n    input_ptr_or_offset: u64,\n    /// Byte offset from the output physical buffer base for this round's output.\n    output_offset: u32,\n    /// log2(output_height) for this entry at this round.\n    log_output_height: u8,\n    /// Matrix width (constant across rounds).\n    width: u32,\n    /// 0 = mat_eval, 1 = selector (determines which buffer pair to use).\n    group: u8,\n}\n\n/// Per-round metadata.\n#[repr(C)]\nstruct FoldRoundInfo {\n    /// Offset into the FoldEntry array where this round's entries start.\n    entry_offset: u32,\n    /// Number of foldable entries this round (mat + sel combined).\n    num_entries: u32,\n    /// Maximum output cells across all entries (for grid sizing).\n    max_output_cells: u32,\n}\n\nstruct FoldPlan {\n    /// All entries for all rounds, uploaded once.\n    d_entries: DeviceBuffer<FoldEntry>,\n    /// Per-round info, uploaded once.\n    d_round_info: DeviceBuffer<FoldRoundInfo>,\n    /// Physical buffer base pointers for mat_evals (A and B).\n    mat_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    mat_phys_b: *mut EF,\n    /// Physical buffer base pointers for selectors (A and B).\n    sel_phys_a: *mut EF,\n    sel_phys_b: *mut EF,\n    /// Number of rounds.\n    n_max: usize,\n}\n```\n\n**Builder function** `FoldPlan::build(mat_evals_per_trace, sels_per_trace, n_max, mat_buf_a, mat_buf_b, sel_buf_a, sel_buf_b)`:\n1. Record physical buffer base pointers from buf_a and buf_b BEFORE any swaps.\n2. For each round R in 1..=n_max:\n   a. For mat_evals (flattened): iterate over matrices, filter those with `initial_height >> (R-1) > 1` (foldable). For each foldable matrix:\n      - Round 1: `input_ptr_or_offset` = matrix's current device pointer (absolute)\n      - Round R≥2: `input_ptr_or_offset` = byte offset of this matrix's data within the physical buffer that is the input for round R (= previous round's output buffer)\n      - `output_offset` = cumulative byte offset in the output physical buffer\n      - `log_output_height` = log2(initial_height >> R)\n      - `width` = matrix width\n      - `group` = 0\n   b. For selectors: same as above with `group` = 1 and selector buffer pointers\n   c. Store `FoldRoundInfo` for this round\n3. Upload `d_entries` and `d_round_info` to device (2 H2D transfers total, replacing 112).\n\nThe output offset calculation for round R, entry i:\n```\noffset[R][i] = sum_{j < i, j foldable at round R} (width_j × (height_j >> R))\n```\nThis depends only on initial heights and widths (known at plan build time).\n\nThe input offset for round R ≥ 2 equals the output offset from round R-1 (since the previous output becomes the current input). For round 1, input is absolute pointers.\n\n### Change 2: New fused fold CUDA kernel\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n\nAdd a new kernel:\n```cuda\nstruct FoldEntry {\n    uint64_t input_ptr_or_offset;\n    uint32_t output_offset;\n    uint8_t log_output_height;\n    uint32_t width;\n    uint8_t group; // 0 = mat, 1 = sel\n};\n\nstruct FoldRoundInfo {\n    uint32_t entry_offset;\n    uint32_t num_entries;\n    uint32_t max_output_cells;\n};\n\n__global__ void batch_fold_mle_planned_kernel(\n    const FoldEntry *entries,\n    const FoldRoundInfo *round_info,\n    const FpExt *mat_input_base,   // physical buffer that is INPUT for this round (mat)\n    FpExt *mat_output_base,        // physical buffer that is OUTPUT for this round (mat)\n    const FpExt *sel_input_base,   // same for selectors\n    FpExt *sel_output_base,\n    uint32_t round,                // 1-indexed round number\n    FpExt r_val\n) {\n    FoldRoundInfo ri = round_info[round - 1];\n    uint32_t mat_idx = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;\n    if (mat_idx >= ri.num_entries) return;\n\n    FoldEntry e = entries[ri.entry_offset + mat_idx];\n\n    // Determine input and output pointers\n    const FpExt *input;\n    FpExt *output;\n    if (e.group == 0) {\n        input = (round == 1)\n            ? reinterpret_cast<const FpExt *>(e.input_ptr_or_offset)\n            : mat_input_base + (e.input_ptr_or_offset / sizeof(FpExt));\n        output = mat_output_base + (e.output_offset / sizeof(FpExt));\n    } else {\n        input = (round == 1)\n            ? reinterpret_cast<const FpExt *>(e.input_ptr_or_offset)\n            : sel_input_base + (e.input_ptr_or_offset / sizeof(FpExt));\n        output = sel_output_base + (e.output_offset / sizeof(FpExt));\n    }\n\n    uint32_t tidx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    // Standard fold: output[j] = input[j] + r * (input[j + half_h * w] - input[j])\n    sumcheck::fold_mle_from_ptrs(input, output, e.width, e.log_output_height, r_val, tidx);\n}\n```\n\nA thin helper `fold_mle_from_ptrs` extracts the fold logic from the existing `sumcheck::fold_mle` to accept direct pointers instead of pointer-to-pointer arrays.\n\n**Launcher:**\n```cuda\nextern \"C\" int _batch_fold_mle_planned(\n    const FoldEntry *entries,\n    const FoldRoundInfo *round_info,\n    const FpExt *mat_input_base,\n    FpExt *mat_output_base,\n    const FpExt *sel_input_base,\n    FpExt *sel_output_base,\n    uint32_t round,\n    uint32_t max_output_cells,\n    uint32_t num_entries,\n    FpExt r_val\n) {\n    auto [grid, block] = fold_mle_launch_params(max_output_cells, num_entries);\n    batch_fold_mle_planned_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n        entries, round_info,\n        mat_input_base, mat_output_base,\n        sel_input_base, sel_output_base,\n        round, r_val\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Change 3: FFI binding\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/mod.rs`\n\nAdd extern declaration and safe wrapper for `_batch_fold_mle_planned`. The wrapper takes `&FoldPlan`, `round: u32`, `r_val: EF` and computes the correct physical buffer pair based on round parity:\n- Round 1 (odd): mat output = mat_phys_b, sel output = sel_phys_b\n- Round R even: mat input = mat_phys_b, mat output = mat_phys_a (and symmetric for sel)\n- Round R odd (R > 1): mat input = mat_phys_a, mat output = mat_phys_b\n\n### Change 4: Modify fold_mle_evals to use FoldPlan\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\n1. Add `fold_plan: Option<FoldPlan>` field to `LogupZerocheckGpu`.\n2. After pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), build the FoldPlan:\n   ```rust\n   prover.fold_plan = Some(FoldPlan::build(\n       &prover.mat_evals_per_trace,\n       &prover.sels_per_trace,\n       n_max,\n       &prover.fold_mat_buf_a, &prover.fold_mat_buf_b,\n       &prover.fold_sel_buf_a, &prover.fold_sel_buf_b,\n   )?);\n   ```\n3. In `fold_mle_evals`, replace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with a single `batch_fold_mle_planned` call using the pre-built plan.\n4. After the kernel, swap both buffer pairs: `swap(mat_buf_a, mat_buf_b)` and `swap(sel_buf_a, sel_buf_b)`.\n5. Update the `DeviceMatrix` views in `mat_evals_per_trace` and `sels_per_trace` to point into the swapped buf_a using pre-computed offsets from the plan.\n\n### Change 5: Keep the old fold_pingpong as fallback\n\n**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nRetain the existing `fold_pingpong` for the case where `fold_plan.is_none()` (e.g., when n_max is 0 or total cells are 0). The plan is used only when both mat and sel buffers are allocated and n_max > 0.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Fold correctness**: For each matrix at each round, `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` must be preserved exactly. The planned kernel computes the same operation with pointers derived from the layout table instead of per-round host arrays.\n\n2. **Buffer ownership**: After fold_mle_evals, `mat_evals_per_trace` views must point to valid data in the current `buf_a`. The plan's pre-computed offsets must match the actual buffer layout at each round.\n\n3. **Non-foldable matrix views**: Matrices that have reached height=1 keep their existing views (pointing into whichever buffer they last landed in). The planned kernel skips them (they're not in the fold entries for that round). The Rust-side view update must also skip them.\n\n4. **Physical buffer identity**: The plan stores the raw physical buffer pointers captured at build time. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps the `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but does NOT change the physical GPU addresses. The kernel uses the physical addresses directly, so swaps are invisible to it — the round number determines which physical buffer is input/output.\n\n5. **Proof correctness**: The fold parameter `r_round` comes from the Fiat-Shamir transcript and is passed as a kernel argument each round. No pre-computation of `r_round` is attempted.\n\n6. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, with ~20 AIRs per segment, the plan build cost (~20 entries × 14 rounds) is negligible. The plan is still beneficial (eliminates 28 kernel launches) but the absolute savings are smaller.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300}\n2. Compare with spec.py:\n   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, target: <150ms)\n   - **Secondary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (current: 1119ms)\n   - **Regression check**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 (current: 1800ms, must not increase by >20ms)\n3. Verify proof correctness: all configs must prove + verify successfully with `--recursion`\n4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in MLE rounds (expect ~14 fold launches per segment instead of ~28)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 10ms (below measurement noise)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 20ms\n- Any proof verification failure\n- Any CUDA error (cudaErrorIllegalAddress, OOM)\n"}}
