{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYTdUG54Py3qc8KkiBf5kG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Pre-compute MLE Fold Layout\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce per-round overhead in MLE fold by: (1) pre-computing fold descriptor arrays for all rounds and bulk-uploading once before the MLE loop, and (2) fusing the two per-round `fold_pingpong` calls (mat_evals + selectors) into a single kernel launch. This replaces 112 per-round `to_device()` calls and 28 kernel launches per segment with 8 bulk uploads and 14 launches.\n6\t\n7\t**Savings estimate**: 5-15ms at APC 300 based on measured per-call overhead of ~0.3-0.5μs (from `bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers` report) and ~0.3ms inter-kernel gap per launch. The fold descriptor overhead is a small fraction of MLE Rounds (166ms), which is dominated by kernel execution time (~113ms). This optimization targets the remaining ~53ms overhead budget.\n8\t\n9\t**Why this is worth doing despite modest savings**: The MLE Rounds anti-scaling (0.71x ratio, 166ms at APC 300 vs 118ms at APC 0) is the single largest scaling bottleneck. Even a 10ms improvement compounds: it brings the APC 0/APC 300 ratio from 1.61x to 1.62x. More importantly, eliminating per-round fold overhead creates a cleaner baseline for future MLE round optimizations (which have been hard to isolate due to noise from this overhead).\n10\t\n11\t## Current Code Path\n12\t\n13\t### Pingpong buffer allocation (mod.rs:622-647)\n14\tAfter Round 0 completes, 4 `DeviceBuffer<EF>` are allocated:\n15\t- `fold_mat_buf_a`, `fold_mat_buf_b` — pingpong pair for evaluation matrices\n16\t- `fold_sel_buf_a`, `fold_sel_buf_b` — pingpong pair for selectors\n17\t\n18\t### MLE rounds loop (mod.rs:660-677)\n19\tEach round calls `fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)`.\n20\t\n21\t### fold_mle_evals (mod.rs:2051-2204)\n22\tCalls `fold_pingpong` twice:\n23\t1. Lines 2147-2168: Fold `mat_evals_per_trace` (flattened `Vec<Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>>`)\n24\t2. Lines 2179-2191: Fold `sels_per_trace` (`Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>`)\n25\t\n26\tBetween these, lines 2169-2177 update `memory_limit_bytes` when `save_memory` is true.\n27\t\n28\t### fold_pingpong (mod.rs:2058-2145)\n29\tEach invocation:\n30\t1. **CPU collection** (lines 2069-2097): Iterates foldable matrices to build 4 arrays: `input_ptrs`, `output_ptrs`, `log_heights`, `widths`.\n31\t2. **H2D upload** (lines 2099-2102): 4× `to_device()` — allocates DeviceBuffer (cudaMallocAsync) + copies.\n32\t3. **Kernel launch** (lines 2104-2114): `batch_fold_mle(...)`.\n33\t4. **Buffer swap** (line 2118): `std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b)`.\n34\t5. **View reconstruction** (lines 2122-2142): Non-owning `DeviceMatrix` views into swapped buf_a.\n35\t6. **Implicit cleanup**: 4 DeviceBuffers dropped (cudaFreeAsync).\n36\t\n37\t### Per-segment costs at APC 300\n38\t- 14 rounds × 2 fold calls × (4 to_device + 1 kernel + 4 drops) = **112 to_device + 28 kernels + 112 drops = 252 CUDA API calls per segment**\n39\t\n40\t### CUDA kernel (sumcheck.cu:192-207, 304-318)\n41\t`batch_fold_mle_kernel` uses `blockIdx.y` for matrix index. Reads per-matrix pointers and dimensions from device arrays. Launcher uses `fold_mle_launch_params` for 2D grid sizing.\n42\t\n43\t### Pingpong buffer invariant\n44\tAfter `fold_pingpong`, buf_a always contains the latest output. Physically:\n45\t- Round R output goes to phys_B (the pre-swap buf_b), then swap makes buf_a = phys_B.\n46\t- So input for round R+1 is always in the physical buffer that round R wrote to.\n47\t- Parity: round R writes to phys_{B if R odd, A if R even}.\n48\t\n49\t## Changes\n50\t\n51\t### Change 1: Pre-compute fold descriptor arrays (Rust)\n52\t\n53\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n54\t\n55\tAfter the pingpong buffer allocation (line 647), add a pre-computation step:\n56\t\n57\t```rust\n58\t// Pre-compute fold descriptors for all rounds.\n59\t// For each round R in 1..=n_max and for each fold group (mat, sel):\n60\t//   - Which matrices are foldable (height >> (R-1) > 1)\n61\t//   - Input pointers (round 1: original matrix ptrs; round R>=2: offset in pingpong buf)\n62\t//   - Output offsets in the alternate pingpong buffer\n63\t//   - log_heights and widths\n64\t\n65\tstruct PrecomputedFold {\n66\t    /// Flat buffer of input pointers for all rounds, indexed by round_offsets.\n67\t    all_input_ptrs: Vec<*const EF>,      // round 1: absolute ptrs; R>=2: cast from buf base + offset\n68\t    all_output_ptrs: Vec<*mut EF>,\n69\t    all_log_heights: Vec<u8>,\n70\t    all_widths: Vec<u32>,\n71\t    /// Per-round: (start_index, num_foldable, max_output_cells) for mat and sel groups.\n72\t    round_meta: Vec<FoldRoundMeta>,\n73\t    /// Physical base pointers captured at build time.\n74\t    mat_phys_a: *mut EF,\n75\t    mat_phys_b: *mut EF,\n76\t    sel_phys_a: *mut EF,\n77\t    sel_phys_b: *mut EF,\n78\t}\n79\t\n80\tstruct FoldRoundMeta {\n81\t    mat_start: usize,\n82\t    mat_count: u16,\n83\t    mat_max_cells: u32,\n84\t    sel_start: usize,\n85\t    sel_count: u16,\n86\t    sel_max_cells: u32,\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\tThe builder iterates over rounds 1..=n_max. For each round:\n91\t- Determines which matrices are foldable based on initial heights\n92\t- For round 1: input_ptrs = absolute device pointers from current DeviceMatrix objects\n93\t- For round R ≥ 2: input_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset matching previous round's output layout\n94\t- Output_ptrs = phys_buf_base + cumulative offset for this round's foldable set\n95\t- The output offset for trace i at round R = `sum_{j<i, foldable at R} (width_j × (initial_height_j >> R))`\n96\t\n97\t**Critical invariant**: The output offsets at round R must match the input offsets at round R+1 exactly. Both use the same iteration order (sorted by height descending, matching the existing code's `partition_point` ordering).\n98\t\n99\tAfter building the CPU arrays, upload them in 4 bulk to_device() calls (one each for input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_heights, widths). These are indexed by round offset during the MLE loop.\n100\t\n101\t### Change 2: Fuse mat+sel fold into single kernel launch\n102\t\n103\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n104\t\n105\tNo new kernel needed. Instead, concatenate the mat and sel descriptor entries into the same arrays:\n106\t- For round R: entries [0..mat_count) are mat_evals, entries [mat_count..mat_count+sel_count) are selectors\n107\t- The existing `batch_fold_mle_kernel` processes all entries uniformly (it doesn't care about the semantic group)\n108\t- Each entry has the correct input/output pointers pointing to the appropriate physical buffer\n109\t\n110\tThe key insight: the existing kernel already processes matrices by index (`blockIdx.y`). By concatenating mat and sel entries, one kernel launch handles both groups.\n111\t\n112\t### Change 3: Modify fold_mle_evals to use pre-computed arrays\n113\t\n114\t**File:** `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n115\t\n116\tReplace the two `fold_pingpong` calls with:\n117\t\n118\t```rust\n119\tfn fold_mle_evals_precomputed(&mut self, round: usize, r_round: EF) -> Result<(), ...> {\n120\t    let plan = self.fold_plan.as_ref().unwrap();\n121\t    let meta = &plan.round_meta[round - 1];\n122\t    let total = meta.mat_count as usize + meta.sel_count as usize;\n123\t    if total == 0 { return Ok(()); }\n124\t\n125\t    let combined_start = meta.mat_start; // mat and sel are concatenated\n126\t    let max_cells = max(meta.mat_max_cells, meta.sel_max_cells);\n127\t\n128\t    // Single kernel launch for both mat + sel\n129\t    unsafe {\n130\t        batch_fold_mle(\n131\t            &plan.d_all_input_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n132\t            &plan.d_all_output_ptrs.slice(combined_start, total),\n133\t            &plan.d_all_widths.slice(combined_start, total),\n134\t            total as u16,\n135\t            &plan.d_all_log_heights.slice(combined_start, total),\n136\t            max_cells,\n137\t            r_round,\n138\t        )?;\n139\t    }\n140\t\n141\t    // Swap both buffer pairs (matches the fold_pingpong behavior)\n142\t    if meta.mat_count > 0 {\n143\t        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_mat_buf_a, &mut self.fold_mat_buf_b) {\n144\t            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n145\t        }\n146\t    }\n147\t    if meta.sel_count > 0 {\n148\t        if let (Some(a), Some(b)) = (&mut self.fold_sel_buf_a, &mut self.fold_sel_buf_b) {\n149\t            std::mem::swap(a, b);\n150\t        }\n151\t    }\n152\t\n153\t    // Reconstruct views using pre-computed offsets (same logic as fold_pingpong lines 2122-2142)\n154\t    // ... rebuild mat_evals_per_trace and sels_per_trace views from swapped buf_a\n155\t    // using the pre-computed output offsets for this round\n156\t\n157\t    // save_memory update (same as lines 2169-2177)\n158\t    if self.save_memory { ... }\n159\t\n160\t    // eq_xis trim + eq_ns update (same as lines 2193-2203)\n161\t    ...\n162\t}\n163\t```\n164\t\n165\t**Note on DeviceBuffer::slice**: The pre-uploaded arrays are indexed by offset. A `slice(start, len)` view into the DeviceBuffer provides the correct sub-range without new allocation. If `DeviceBuffer` doesn't support slicing, pass base pointer + offset as raw pointers.\n166\t\n167\t### Change 4: Fallback path\n168\t\n169\tKeep the existing `fold_pingpong` code for when `fold_plan.is_none()` (n_max = 0 or no foldable matrices).\n170\t\n171\t## Invariants\n172\t\n173\t1. **Fold arithmetic**: `output[j] = input[j] + r × (input[j + half_h × w] - input[j])` preserved exactly — same kernel, same inputs, same outputs.\n174\t\n175\t2. **Buffer pointer identity**: The pre-computed plan captures physical buffer pointers BEFORE any swaps. The `std::mem::swap` on the Rust side swaps `DeviceBuffer` wrappers but physical GPU addresses don't change. The plan's pointers always refer to physical addresses.\n176\t\n177\t3. **Offset thread-through**: Output offsets at round R define the data layout. Input offsets at round R+1 must match exactly. Both are computed from the same sorted iteration order (height descending) with the same foldability criteria.\n178\t\n179\t4. **Non-foldable view preservation**: Matrices that have reached height=1 are not in any round's descriptor entries. Their `DeviceMatrix` views remain unchanged across rounds, pointing to wherever they last landed (matching the existing `extend_from_slice(&input_mats[num_foldable..])` behavior).\n180\t\n181\t5. **save_memory ordering**: The `memory_limit_bytes` update (lines 2169-2177) must see the NEW mat_evals views before computing sizes. In the fused approach, both mat and sel fold happen in one kernel, so the view reconstruction for mat_evals MUST precede the save_memory update. This matches the current sequencing (mat fold → save_memory → sel fold) because the save_memory update only reads mat_evals, not sel.\n182\t\n183\t6. **Proof correctness**: Only the fold parameter `r_round` changes per round. All pre-computed data depends on static trace metadata (initial heights, widths, pointer addresses), not on transcript challenges.\n184\t\n185\t## Measurement Plan\n186\t\n187\t1. Run `run_pairing.sh` benchmarks for APC {0, 100, 300}\n188\t2. Compare with spec.py:\n189\t   - **Primary metric**: MLE Rounds at APC 300 (current: 166ms, expect: 155-161ms)\n190\t   - **Secondary metric**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 (current: 1119ms)\n191\t   - **Regression check**: STARK excl trace at APC 0 (current: 1800ms, must not increase by >20ms)\n192\t3. Verify: all configs prove + verify with `--recursion`\n193\t4. Profile with nsight: confirm reduced kernel launch count in fold phase (14 per segment vs 28)\n194\t\n195\t## Rollback Criteria\n196\t\n197\t- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 < 3ms (below measurement noise floor, indicating zero real impact)\n198\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 20ms\n199\t- Any proof verification failure\n200\t- Any CUDA error\n201\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0195TSfhzKAqubPTtiSKo625","session_id":"56bc24eb-d9a0-4ad2-8690-031f38c3d9d4","uuid":"f42fc071-907c-4737-8988-1975f63c62e6","timestamp":"2026-04-15T19:14:26.723Z"}
